 standpoint we know that speed sells  provided that  the the revenue uplift that you're  expecting from it isn't too important so  this is back to the premium point that  we were uh highlighting i am absolutely  convinced that if your gigabit offer  is 20 more expensive than your 100 meg  offer and you do a concerted marketing  effort to get people to upsell you'll  get great success on the upsell  so that's one aspect right so it's  really independent of what people are  doing it  doing with it to a certain extent now of  course we are anticipating down the line  some applications that will require  either  very high capacity or  very low latency or both so  you know vr et cetera for sure now the  the the question mark that i have is  um  do we  anticipate that 50 of households are  gonna have vr sets within  five years i don't see it  i may be wrong and it'd be great for  vendors and you know and and uh  application providers if i was wrong but  i just don't see it so  um you don't build your network strategy  on the basis of  uh you know  a niche market you you you build it on  the basis of what most consumers are  going to want to buy which is a  different thing than what they need  right remember we don't need  uh 1200 dollar handsets we would be  doing 95 of what we're doing with 300  handsets  but people are buying 1200 handsets  because they want to so from a marketing  standpoint i don't think building the  network strategy on an understanding of  what the need is  is where you need to be you need to look  at what your internal needs are where  are you saturating and i know from  having talked to a lot of operators that  they see their pawn saturating in some  areas  and this is what drives the adoption of  xgs pawn  once you have xgspawn then marketing can  take over and say okay what should i  sell 2gig five gig  whatever and how do i sell it but  there's not i think you know 8k tv it'll  come but all of these things will  actually the ecosystem will evolve  afterwards not before i suppose is what  i'm saying so i think the key drivers  are going to happen  once the infrastructure is at least  partly in place and actually this allows  me to touch upon a point that a lot of  people dismiss but i think is important  uh remember how  when we were all on dial-up and then a  few  you know privileged people started  migrating to dsl and they were saying oh  my god everything is so fast and then  within a few years  everything was not so fast anymore and  dial-up was unusable  that's because the entire online  ecosystem adjusted to hey everybody's  got dsl now  and  gradually slowly the same thing is  happening  with  every website out there now right the  the basic assumption is that you have  fiber speeds  at least 100 make if not more and so  your online experience if you're still  on dsl today or if you're on a 50 meg  package your online experience is now  degrading just like dial-up was  degrading 20 years ago  um the the shift is more gradual but  it's still  happening and so  i think as  as service providers  uh  or no well i'm not a service provider  service providers need to  uh to realize that part of the  uh evolution of their own offers is also  following that trend so there's a very  interesting example with chorus in new  zealand for example last december  they uh they basically increased the  speeds of their basic packages without  charging more  just because they need to adjust to the  fact that people are now using these  basic packages differently and so the  purpose is not an upsell it's just to  deliver a service that allows people to  do what they wanted to do with it  because they don't feel like their use  has changed that much  but the need from a network standpoint  did and so i think that's very smart  thinking this is how you keep your  customers as well  exactly exactly  now you mentioned the network um and as  a last question i would like to say um  we talk about more and more speed for  consumers we talk about bigger bit  monetization gigabit speed  um but that additional capacity that you  have then  in the network does that not compromise  if you talk about these other services  or other segments for business or for  mobile transport or whatever on your  file infrastructure  so so what's what's really interesting  and this is we're really at the very  beginning of that trend right but what's  really interesting is that as these  higher uh  uh  speed network technologies are being  implemented xgs pawn 25g  also in parallel we see  uh  the the virtualization of the network uh  becoming more and more of a reality and  that opens up a very important  capability when it comes to fixed  networks which is fixed network slicing  slicing is a term that's used a lot in  the mobile space but actually in the  fixed space it has very  clear and practical applications in  order to basically  shell i suppose could be a word or  tunnel specific  uh  traffics within the network to to  shelter them from  excessive usage so if you are using your  xgspawn network for mobile backhoe  you can use slicing to ensure that that  mobile backhoe will not be affected by  anything else that happens on the  network and this is a way to effectively  replicate  multiple physical networks without  having to deploy multiple physical  networks and so the way that i see this  is that this is the only way that this  one network vision can actually  become a reality because we still need  from a logical standpoint to be able to  separate the business market from the  mobile backhaul or mobile front hole  and from the residential customers um  and and slicing is actually the way to  do that and it's interesting you know  these  trends in in technology because a few  years ago  like everybody was talking about slicing  now it's like nobody's talking about it  anymore but it's still there it's still  happening and it really is a very  important step that uh you know we need  to  keep in mind and operators need to  put a lot of  uh thought process into because the the  big benefit of this one network vision  is going to be the ability to switch off  the copper to switch off the dedicated  fiber links to  mobile masts once you can really manage  this as a single network i mean some  operators are managing effectively four  or five networks in parallel the opex  and energy cost of that not to mention  the sustainability angle it's absurd we  absolutely need to have that as a  priority agenda for operators  absolutely  and with that as an excellent uh closing  i would like to close this session  um i don't see any additional questions  anymore  well thank you very much  yes  thank you  yeah i was gonna say thanks to everyone  who attended and uh obviously feel free  to uh reach out if you have any  additional questions uh don't forget to  download the the white paper  and uh and thanks to you eric and to the  council for uh hosting this it was a  pleasure thank you very much and see you  next time bye
 

